 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Deliver committed changes to browsers over persistent WebSocket connections for low-latency updates (targeting subsecond, supporting up to 20 edits/sec during opening-time bursts) without recurring vendor fees or provider coupling. The tradeoff is owning connection capacity, session management, and reconnect resilience instead of delegating those operations to a hosted provider. Five-second HTTPS polling was simpler but cannot meet the latency target. Hosted realtime services (Pusher, Ably, Firebase) would provide faster time-to-market but incur recurring costs and lock the board to the provider's uptime SLA.
